 "title": "Monster sorter",
 "body": "<p><strong>Goal:</strong> build a tiny machine-learning program that sorts monsters into <strong>friendly</strong> and <strong>grumpy</strong> by learning from example cards.</p>\n<p>Each card has two <strong>features</strong> - <code>fluff</code> and <code>teeth</code> (0 to 10) - and a <strong>label</strong>. To guess a mystery monster, find the training card that is <strong>nearest</strong> on the chart and copy its label.</p>\n<p><strong>Steps</strong></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Open <code>starter.py</code>. Look at the <code>TRAINING</code> cards at the top.</li>\n<li>TODO 1: finish <code>distance(a, b)</code> with Pythagoras: <code>math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy)</code>.</li>\n<li>TODO 2: finish <code>nearest(training, mystery)</code> - keep the closest card so far, just like finding the smallest number in a list.</li>\n<li>Press <strong>Run</strong>. The chart shows friendly cards (circle, F) and grumpy cards (square, G). Each mystery monster (white circle, ?) has a line to its nearest neighbor and a guess.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Done looks like:</strong> <code>(5, 2) -&gt; friendly</code> and <code>(3, 6) -&gt; grumpy</code>. What does the program guess for <code>(5, 5)</code>? Do you agree?</p>\n<p><strong>Extra:</strong> add your own mystery monsters to <code>MYSTERY</code>.</p>\n",
